# claudebox settings for this machine. Copy to "config" next to this file:
#
#   cp ~/.claudebox/config.example ~/.claudebox/config
#
# Sourced as shell by the launcher, so it is plain assignments (and anything
# else bash accepts, though there is rarely a reason). The environment wins over
# what is set here, so a one-off override still works:
#
#   BOX_WORKSPACE_HOST=/mnt/elsewhere claudebox
#
# "config" is gitignored: it describes this machine, not the box's design.

# --- the workspace ---------------------------------------------------------

# Where the box sees the source tree. Leave this alone unless you have a reason:
# everything in the box, and every path it prints, is built on it.
#BOX_WORKSPACE="${HOME}/Projects"

# The host directory bound there. Defaults to BOX_WORKSPACE, which keeps paths
# identical inside and out. Point it elsewhere to work on a tree that lives on
# another drive -- an external SSD, or a removable drive carried between
# machines -- while the box still sees a plain ~/Projects:
#BOX_WORKSPACE_HOST=/media/dizzy/transfer/Projects

# --- everything else -------------------------------------------------------

# The box user and its ids. Default to yours; changing them means rebuilding.
#BOX_USER="$(id -un)"
#BOX_UID="$(id -u)"
#BOX_GID="$(id -g)"

# Which host home the launcher reads optional config from (~/.gitconfig,
# opencode's config and credentials).
#BOX_HOST_HOME="${HOME}"

# The mkcert root CA to trust, so https://*.ddev.site validates in the box.
#BOX_MKCERT_CA="${HOME}/.local/share/mkcert/rootCA.pem"

# Which DDEV project's database is reachable as db:3306. Auto-detected when
# exactly one project is running; pin it when several are.
#BOX_DDEV_PROJECT=matomo

# Where "claudebox <plugin>:<command>" looks, colon-separated, earliest wins.
# The default is claudebox's own plugins/, which is where the plugins it ships
# live. Setting this REPLACES that, so append it when adding a place to look
# rather than moving -- a plugin of your own, or one kept in its own checkout.
#BOX_PLUGIN_PATH="${HOME}/my-plugins:${BOX_DIR}/plugins"

# AppArmor profile for the rootless dind sidecar. On Ubuntu 24.04 the named
# profile is required -- see apparmor/claudebox-dind. On a host with no AppArmor
# confinement, "unconfined" is right.
#BOX_DIND_APPARMOR=unconfined

# --- the agent door ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# A runner machine is the box MINUS the agent, on a network with no egress
# allowlist, so it is the wrong machine to run claude on. It does not: every
# claudebox command there refuses, except a headless one-off, which is sent to
# a workstation and answered in ITS box.
#
# On the WORKSTATION -- the machine that runs the agent. Off by default; setting
# this creates the front door (agent-relay), publishes the port below, and
# starts a listener in the box behind it.
#
# "claudebox agent start" writes this for you, brings both up and opens an
# enrolment window, which is easier than setting it here and restarting by hand.
#BOX_AGENT_SERVE=1
#BOX_AGENT_PORT=7996

# On a RUNNER MACHINE -- set by "claudebox agent add <host:port> <code>", using
# the code the workstation's "claudebox agent enrol" prints. The mirror of the
# BOX_RUNNER trio below, pointed the other way: an address, a bearer token and
# the certificate fingerprint to pin. They are handed to a one-off container and
# to nothing else.
#
# Edit them by re-running "claudebox agent add" rather than by hand; the
# fingerprint has to match the workstation's current certificate exactly.
#BOX_AGENT=192.168.1.10:7996
#BOX_AGENT_TOKEN=...
#BOX_AGENT_FINGERPRINT=AA:BB:...

# --- the remote test runner -------------------------------------------------

# Set by "claudebox runner add <host:port> <code>", using the eight-character
# enrolment code the other machine's "claudebox-runner" prints. They are
# handed to the runner-relay container and to nothing else -- the box itself
# never sees the address, the token or the fingerprint, which is what keeps a
# remote runner from being a hole in the egress sandbox.
#
# Edit them by re-running "claudebox runner add" rather than by hand; the
# fingerprint has to match the runner's current certificate exactly.
#BOX_RUNNER=192.168.1.42:7998
#BOX_RUNNER_TOKEN=...
#BOX_RUNNER_FINGERPRINT=AA:BB:...

# --- on a RUNNER machine ----------------------------------------------------
#
# The same file is read by "claudebox-runner" on the machine that runs the
# tests, where these are the settings that apply instead. A machine is normally
# one or the other, so these sit unused on this one.

# The port the runner listens on, and where it keeps its copy of the source
# (a path inside its workspace volume, not on that machine's disk).
#BOX_RUNNER_PORT=7998
#BOX_RUNNER_WORKSPACE=/home/dizzy/Projects

# Where the DDEV router's plain-HTTP entrypoint goes inside the runner's dind.
# Port 80 in there belongs to the host.docker.internal front door, which is the
# address and the port a single-host CI runner serves Matomo on -- moving it
# would put a port suffix into URLs the screenshot baselines contain. https is
# unaffected and stays on 443. Change this only if 8080 collides.
#BOX_RUNNER_ROUTER_HTTP_PORT=8080

# --- the workspace over ssh -------------------------------------------------
#
# Two machines, one source tree. On the machine that HAS the tree, "claudebox
# ssh start" brings up a container running sshd over the same bind the box gets
# and publishes it on the LAN; on the other, "claudebox ssh mount <host>" mounts
# it with sshfs and points BOX_WORKSPACE_HOST at the mount, so claudebox there
# runs against this tree while the box still sees a plain ~/Projects.
#
# Keys only -- there is no password, and no token. A machine joins by having its
# public key added over here with "claudebox ssh authorize", which is the line
# its own "claudebox ssh mount" prints.

# On the machine that SERVES the workspace. Written by "claudebox ssh start",
# which also brings the door up -- easier than setting it here and starting by
# hand.
#BOX_SSH_SERVE=1
#BOX_SSH_PORT=7994

# On the machine that MOUNTS one -- set by "claudebox ssh mount". The address
# joined, and the host key fingerprint pinned there; a machine that later
# answers with a different key is refused rather than prompted about.
#
# Edit these by re-running "claudebox ssh mount" rather than by hand; the
# fingerprint has to match the far side's current host key exactly.
#BOX_SSH_REMOTE=dizzy@192.168.1.10:7994
#BOX_SSH_FINGERPRINT=SHA256:...

# What BOX_WORKSPACE_HOST was before the mount took it over. Written by
# "claudebox ssh mount" and read by "claudebox ssh eject", which is the only
# thing that puts the local workspace back -- so this is not a setting to write
# by hand.
#BOX_SSH_LOCAL_WORKSPACE_HOST=/home/dizzy/Projects
